{"name":"Constellation Blue","hex":"#3c4670","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"constellation","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An arbitrary formation of stars perceived as a figure (especially one from mythology) or pattern, or a division of the sky including it, especially one officially recognized by astronomers; an asterism.\n\n Any of the 88 regions of the sky officially recognized by the International Astronomical Union, including all stars and celestial bodies in the region. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any of the 88 regions of the sky officially recognized by the International Astronomical Union, including all stars and celestial bodies in the region. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/constellation"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Constellation","blocks":[{"text":"A constellation is an area on the celestial sphere in which a group of visible stars forms a perceived pattern or outline, typically representing an animal, mythological subject, or inanimate object."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Orion_constellation_-_Prodromus_astronomiae_1690_%285590584%29.jpg/330px-Orion_constellation_-_Prodromus_astronomiae_1690_%285590584%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constellation"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Book_of_the_Fixed_Stars_Auv0333_Orion.jpg/500px-Book_of_the_Fixed_Stars_Auv0333_Orion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Book_of_the_Fixed_Stars_Auv0333_Orion.jpg/1280px-Book_of_the_Fixed_Stars_Auv0333_Orion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Book_of_the_Fixed_Stars_Auv0333_Orion.jpg/500px-Book_of_the_Fixed_Stars_Auv0333_Orion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABook_of_the_Fixed_Stars_Auv0333_Orion.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/OrionCC.jpg/330px-OrionCC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/OrionCC.jpg/330px-OrionCC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrionCC.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedi